  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Metformin remains the traditional first-line oral medication for type 2 diabetes, while SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists are recommended as first-line or early add-on choices primarily for patients with specific comorbid conditions (such as heart failure, chronic kidney disease, or atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease).",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Metformin remains the traditional first-line oral medication"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Major guidelines from organizations like the American Diabetes Association (ADA) guide the choice between SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 RAs based on individual patient profile rather than a one-size-fits-all first-line rule.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Glycemic Control & Weight Loss:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists provide superior HbA1c reduction (typically 0.7% to 1.7%) and more pronounced weight loss compared to SGLT2 inhibitors."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors offer more modest HbA1c reductions (0.3% to 1.2%) and moderate weight loss (1.5 to 3 kg)."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heart Failure & Kidney Protection:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors are the clear preference for patients with heart failure (reducing heart failure hospitalizations) and chronic kidney disease (slowing hard renal endpoints and eGFR decline)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists also provide cardiovascular and renal benefits (such as reducing major adverse cardiovascular events and albuminuria), but are less potent for heart failure with preserved or reduced ejection fraction compared to SGLT2 inhibitors."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4,
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Route of Administration:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors are taken orally once daily."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists are primarily administered via subcutaneous injection (daily or weekly), though oral semaglutide is available."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors carry risks of genitourinary (yeast/UTI) infections and euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists commonly cause gastrointestinal side effects like nausea or vomiting, especially during dose escalation."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you share whether the patient has heart disease, kidney disease, or struggles with weight, I can help outline which class aligns best with current clinical guidelines."
      }
    ],
